## Chapter Eight: The Theory of Varṇa Mobility — Grammar and Evidence

This is the chapter that most directly contradicts the dominant reading of the Manusmṛti. It draws on close grammatical analysis of three verses and on living village evidence that the system they describe was operationally real within recent memory.

### The Three Verses

**Manu 10.65:**
> *śūdro brāhmaṇatām eti brāhmaṇaścaiti śūdratām*
> "A śūdra attains the state of brahminhood; a brāhmaṇa descends to the state of a śūdra."

The grammar is decisive. The verb *eti* is the third person singular present of the root *i* — to go, to attain, to move toward. Combined with *brāhmaṇatām*, an abstract accusative formed with the suffix *-tā* (designating a state or quality), the construction is the standard Sanskrit idiom for *becoming* a state. It is the verb of motion toward a destination.

You cannot grammatically move toward a destination you already permanently occupy by birth. The motion verb presupposes that brahminhood is a state to be reached, not one already given. The abstract noun suffix *-tā* further encodes brahminhood as a property that can be acquired — not an inherited essence or natural kind.

The verse is chiastic: śūdra-to-brahminhood and brahmin-to-śūdra-hood are grammatically parallel. Both directions are equally possible. Pure blood cannot revert. If brahminhood were in the blood, no failure of conduct could remove it within a generation. Manu's grammar says otherwise.

**Manu 10.121:**
> *śūdrastu vṛttimākāṅkṣan kṣatramārādhayedyadi*
> "If a śūdra, desiring livelihood, should serve the kṣatriya..."

The three tiers of service are established: serve a kṣatriya for survival, a wealthy vaiśya for prosperity, a brāhmaṇa for transcendence. The absolutive *upārādhya* — 'having served' — encodes each tier as a completed prerequisite before the next applies. The desiderative verb *jijīviṣet* — 'he should be in the disposition of wishing to live' — describes not a single action but a whole-life orientation. This cannot be satisfied by a ceremony. It describes a sustained posture across a lifetime.

**Manu 10.122:**
> *jāta-brāhmaṇa-śabdasya sa hy asya kṛtakṛtyatā*
> "That indeed is his complete fulfillment — the arising of the designation 'brahmin' for him."

Three grammatical features are critical. First, *jāta-brāhmaṇa-śabda*: the PPP *jāta* (from *jan*, to be born or to arise) modifies the *śabda* — the word, title, designation — not the person. The title has arisen organically. It has not been assigned. A conferral would use *datta* (given) or *āropita* (placed upon). The use of *jāta* for a title earned through conduct is deliberate and precise.

Second, *kṛtakṛtyatā*: built on the gerundive *kṛtya* (that which ought to be done), this compound names the state of having fulfilled all obligation. A gerundive expresses sustained obligatory action over time, not a momentary event. This is the culmination of a life, not the outcome of an exam.

Third, the whole sequence — service, arising title, complete fulfillment — describes what recent scholarship has called the **jātabrāhmaṇa status**: a person for whom the social designation 'brahmin' has organically emerged through recognised conduct, but who has not yet undergone upanayana (the constitutive second-birth sacrament) and therefore does not yet hold full dvijatva.

### The Two-Generation Model

The three verses together describe a precise model:

| Generation | Person | Mechanism | Outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| First | Śūdra | Lifetime of graduated service culminating in brahminical service | *Jātabrāhmaṇa* — the title arises; dvijatva not yet constituted |
| Second | His children | Upanayana performed | Full dvijatva; brahmin in the ritual-legal sense |
| Failure | Children skip upanayana | Eligibility lapses | Śūdratva restored as default |

This model has several important features:

**It is real but incomplete in the first generation.** The *jātabrāhmaṇa* title is not a fiction or a consolation. It is a genuine social and moral recognition. But it stops precisely at the threshold of the constitutive sacrament. The upanayana must be performed at the appropriate life-stage — it cannot be retroactively performed on an adult who was born śūdra. The crossing is therefore inherently generational.

**It has no permanent resting points in either direction.** A brāhmaṇa family that abandons brahminical conduct does not immediately lose dvijatva — the sacrament cannot be undone — but the title hollows within a generation, and children born into degraded conduct without meaningful upanayana do not inherit operative brāhmaṇa status. Śūdratva is restored as the default within two generations.

**It is structurally symmetrical.** The same logic that allows upward mobility also enforces downward mobility. No family can coast on inherited status for more than one generation without continued conduct.

### Village Evidence

The grammatical model is confirmed by living evidence. In a village in western India, two groups of families share the same surname, gotra, and kuladevatā temple. One group is recognised as brāhmaṇa. The other is a barber community. Both groups actively worship at the same kuladevatā temple and treat each other as family.

Village oral tradition holds that the brāhmaṇa families were elevated from śūdra status some generations ago.

The shared gotra is the decisive evidence. Gotra is strictly patrilineal — it designates the founding ṛṣi of the patrilineal lineage. It cannot be acquired through proximity or social aspiration. It can only be transmitted through birth in the lineage or through formal adoption at a constitutive ceremony — which is precisely what upanayana is. If the barber and brāhmaṇa families share a gotra, they share a patrilineal ancestor from whom the gotra-transmission descended. The *jātabrāhmaṇa* ancestor, whose children underwent upanayana and received the gotra of the guru or sponsoring brāhmaṇa lineage at that ceremony, is the node at which the shared gotra originates.

Those children became brāhmaṇa families. Those who did not undergo upanayana — whose eligibility lapsed — remained barbers. But neither family forgot the common origin. The kuladevatā temple holds the memory.

Most strikingly: the brāhmaṇa families still have their children's *chūḍākaraṇa* — the ritual first haircut, one of the sixteen core saṃskāras — performed by their barber kin. Not by an outside barber. By family.

Every generation's chūḍākaraṇa in this village is an unreflective, unideological performance of Manu 10.121-122 — the barber family serving the brāhmaṇa family in the precise ritual relationship that the text identifies as the mechanism of formation. The village has been living the grammar of Manu's actual text, without knowing it, for as many generations as anyone can remember.

